      "dates": [],
      "descriptions": [
        {
          "description": "Brain metastases are increasingly common due to improved cancer detection and advances in treatments that prolong patient survival, creating a need for improved imaging for monitoring metastatic disease. We present the University of California San Diego Longitudinal Brain Metastases dataset (UCSD-BMETS-Longitudinal), comprising 300 patients with intracranial metastases and a total of 1,065 MRI timepoints, including both pre- and post-treatment imaging. together with neuroradiologist-approved segmentations of metastases are provided in NIfTI format. When available, additional sequences such as T1 post-contrast FSE, diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI and ADC), ASL perfusion, and susceptibility-sensitive sequences are also included. An accompanying spreadsheet provides clinical and demographic information. This dataset will facilitate the development of AI models to advance disease evaluation, longitudinal monitoring, and treatment planning in patients with brain metastases.",
          "descriptionType": "Abstract",
          "lang": null
        }
      ],
